{
 "meta": {
  "name": "Credit Counterfeit Index",
  "abbrev": "CCI",
  "definition": "CCI = Monetary Bind (mean z of HY OAS, SLOOS C&I tightening, real fed funds, inverted real M2 growth) minus Real Bind (genuine physical scarcity intensity). Credit is the counterfeiter when CCI >= threshold: tight money manufacturing scarcity that is not physically real.",
  "threshold": 1,
  "as_of": "2026-08-15",
  "sources": "Fed H.6 (M2, seasonally adjusted); Fed H.15 (effective federal funds rate); Fed Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey, July 2026; BEA core PCE; ICE BofA US High Yield OAS. Real Bind is a published judgment series (Strait of Hormuz transit constraints, EIA Short-Term Energy Outlook). FRED is not reachable from the build environment; series are sourced from the issuing agencies directly.",
  "projection_note": "Entries with \"projected\": true are scenario estimates under the hawkish path, not observations.",
  "revision_note": "2026-08-15 reevaluation. 2026Q2 SLOOS revised from 8 to 3 to reflect the July 2026 survey, which reported C&I standards basically unchanged for firms of all sizes. 2026Q3 is now observed through mid-August rather than projected. Every monetary input came in looser than the July projection and the Real Bind did not fade, so the index moved away from the tipping point rather than toward it.",
  "known_limitation": "The Monetary Bind is built from aggregate inputs and cannot see the distribution beneath them. The July 2026 SLOOS is the clearest illustration: banks eased loan rate spreads for large and middle-market firms while a net share tightened credit card standards, and the aggregate C&I reading was 'basically unchanged'. A distributional companion is proposed but not yet scored, because no consistent history exists back to 2006."
 },
